\section{Define a Transfer Before Encoding States}

A finite-state machine has state, a rule for its next state, and a rule for its outputs. In a Moore model outputs depend only on state; Mealy outputs may also depend on current inputs. For an interface controller, neither label says what counts as delivery. Define that first. Here a source offers a word with \texttt{valid}, a destination advertises capacity with \texttt{ready}, and a transfer occurs on a rising edge sampling both high. Once offered, the source must keep validity and data stable until the transfer. Reset is a separate flush event: this module masks both handshakes while reset is asserted, and a rising edge with reset clears the slot.

Venkataramani, Budiu, Chelcea, and Goldstein's \emph{C to Asynchronous Dataflow Circuits}, section~4, offers a useful contrast. Their clockless bundled-data protocol places data, raises a ready wire, waits for acknowledgment, and then returns both controls to zero. The similarly named wires in our synchronous example do not implement that four-phase protocol: consecutive rising edges may each perform a transfer without an intervening return to zero. Both designs need explicit timing rules relating the data to the control signals.

\section{A One-Entry Buffer with Same-Edge Replacement}

This SystemVerilog model is simulatable and normally synthesizable RTL \cite{ieee1800systemverilog}. The control states are empty and full. The word held in the slot is datapath state. When full, the buffer can accept a new word on the same edge that consumes the old one.

\begin{verbatim}
module elastic1 (
    input  logic       clk, rst,
    input  logic       in_valid,
    input  logic [7:0] in_data,
    output wire        in_ready,
    output wire        out_valid,
    output wire  [7:0] out_data,
    input  logic       out_ready
);
    logic full;
    logic [7:0] saved;
    wire push, pop;

    assign out_valid = !rst && full;
    assign in_ready  = !rst && (!full || out_ready);
    assign out_data  = saved;
    assign push = in_valid && in_ready;
    assign pop  = out_valid && out_ready;

    always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
        if (rst) full <= 1'b0;
        else begin
            case ({push, pop})
                2'b10: full <= 1'b1;
                2'b01: full <= 1'b0;
                default: ;      // keep state on 00 or 11
            endcase
            if (push) saved <= in_data;
        end
    end
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

Outside reset, \texttt{out\_valid} depends on occupancy, not on the consumer. The producer can therefore observe it even if the consumer is not ready. By contrast, \texttt{in\_ready} depends on \texttt{out\_ready} when full. That combinational path is the price of accepting a replacement immediately; composing a chain of such stages increases the ready path length, and wiring feedback can create combinational loops. A buffer with no combinational ready path needs a different capacity or throughput policy. The output data path here never bypasses the slot: an input accepted while empty cannot also be consumed on that same edge.

\Cref{fig:visual-311} shows the control transitions for known values and inactive reset. The slot memory is written only on a push, including a simultaneous push and pop.

